OWNED_BY — whose area this is, durable and never released

Tier 0, on Anthony's word. The THIRD "who" axis, and the one that was missing.

    AUTHORED_BY   who WROTE this          past tense · historical · never changes
    CLAIMS        who is IN it RIGHT NOW  transient · advisory · released at checkout
    OWNED_BY      whose AREA this is      durable · standing · never released

⭐ AN ACCEPTED DECISION'S CONDITION, MET AND CLOSED.
dec:ownership-reads-claims-before-adding-an-edge (accepted 2026-08-08) said
build from CLAIMS first, and "decide on OWNED_BY only after that is built and
SHOWN TO BE INSUFFICIENT" — naming the disqualifying evidence in advance:
"claims are TRANSIENT WORK-IN-HAND while ownership is DURABLE". dev_storyflow
filed exactly that distinction a day later and Anthony supplied the use case:
"areas of the design that are his and areas that are mine... durable, survives
every session, and should never be released."

Claims cannot carry it, operationally rather than theoretically: claim_region's
own description says advisory and session-scoped, the skill says release at
checkout, and on a shared server a claim reads `unknown` and never expires.
Standing ownership claims would drown that tool's actual job.

WHAT SHIPPED
  · OWNED_BY, `* → Contributor`, with `note` and `since`.
  · A served `owned_by` tool.
  · loop_status(contributor_id) reports `gaps_on_owned_ground`, each entry
    naming WHICH owned nodes the gap stands on; `not_attributable` shrinks by
    exactly that many. Scoped `clean` now needs BOTH halves empty — a gap on
    your own ground is owed by you as surely as a decision you were asked to
    settle. Its served description was rewritten to say so (1495/1500 chars):
    a tool returning a field its own description never names is the drift class
    fixed twice already today.
  · NOT a traceability edge — third of a kind after AUTHORED_BY and CLAIMS.
    Owning something says who ANSWERS for it, not that changing it changes them.
    A test asserts a Contributor never appears in a blast radius.

⚠️ DELIBERATELY NOT BUILT: the ownership-orphan detector. An unowned node raises
NOTHING. Most of a mature design legitimately has no owner, so a naive detector
fires on the majority and becomes a signal trained to be ignored — while in a
two-person project the same detector is nearly silent. Which types are OWNABLE
is a per-project input nobody has supplied; it stays open in
dec:idea-detect-ownership-orphans.

THE ASYMMETRY IS THE DESIGN: "is this gap on ground you own?" is a FACT the
graph can answer. "Should this have an owner?" is a JUDGEMENT it cannot.

TESTS carry positive controls throughout: a second contributor who owns nothing
gets an empty list from the identical call — without which an implementation
attributing every gap to everybody would pass every other assertion — plus a
conservation check that scoping never shrinks the design-wide gap count.

⚠️ STAMP MOVED: a 61st edge type, so the next release owes an upgrade note. FOUR
separate count guards caught it — schema.rs, vocabulary.rs, the MCP tools test
and smoke_mcp.py — the schema's own tripwire working as intended.

⚠️ AND THE EXPORT NEEDED A REAL SERVER RESTART, which is a new failure mode
worth recording. `cargo build` replaced the binary the CLIENT process was
running, so its /proc/self/exe read `(deleted)` and it spawned the server from a
path ending in that literal marker — every call failed with "No such file or
directory". `--stop-shared` does NOT fix this: that remedy replaces the DAEMON
and assumes the client's own binary still exists. A `/mcp` reconnect did not fix
it either — it reused the same pid. Killing the stale client and reconnecting
did. Before that, the live server exported a stamp saying 60 edge types with
OWNED_BY ABSENT; this export says 61 with OWNED_BY present, verified by READING
the stamp rather than trusting the write.

1062 tests, fmt, clippy (core + mcp), skill_lint, toolsnap, smoke_mcp,
validate_schema, reflow2_check.

